        ]
      },
      "worked": "The definition $\\cot\\theta=\\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}$ requires only $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$. When $\\cos\\theta=0$, sine is nonzero and cot is zero.\n\nHowever, $\\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$ is undefined when $\\cos\\theta=0$. Thus $\\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta}$ excludes these angles as well as the angles where $\\sin\\theta=0$. For example, $\\cot90^\\circ=0$, but $\\dfrac{1}{\\tan90^\\circ}$ is undefined.\n\nSine and cosine are defined for every real angle. Therefore $\\sec\\theta=\\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}$ and $\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}$ exclude exactly their denominator zeros, with no extra exclusions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
